    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "c6021e04-2262-3379-a9f5-ce226ab59aae",
                "word": "preorder",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4ac562c9-a85d-3057-9db5-5d235efbb541",
                        "entry_id": "c6021e04-2262-3379-a9f5-ce226ab59aae",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Such that, recursively, the root is visited before the left and right subtrees."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "88f1dea1-fe19-43aa-ba20-e6b5cd3301e6",
                                "sense_id": "4ac562c9-a85d-3057-9db5-5d235efbb541",
                                "text": "Now, the preorder traversal of a tree can be constructed from the preorder traversals of the subtrees rooted at the children of the root of the tree."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "077302e8-9eec-42e9-b53d-5a3a931163ff",
                                "sense_id": "4ac562c9-a85d-3057-9db5-5d235efbb541",
                                "text": "In the program given above, tree is constructed and is traversed in inorder, preorder and postorder traversal."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bf1e818c-4477-4d47-8b7e-e56db1f2e77e",
                                "sense_id": "4ac562c9-a85d-3057-9db5-5d235efbb541",
                                "text": "2011, Ananda Rao Akepogu, Radhika Raju Palagiri, Data Structures and Algorithms Using C++, Pearson Education India, page 9.16,\nThe preorder traversal visits a node first after which it traverses its left subtree and then traverses its right subtree."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "4b9dc30c-f8dc-3ac7-a9f4-7a467c6014e1",
                "word": "preorder",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "936befa6-6cee-4b05-9912-002dcc78aa42",
                        "entry_id": "4b9dc30c-f8dc-3ac7-a9f4-7a467c6014e1",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "preorders"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1ef366ce-e3dd-3df4-9172-9690e7d1e45d",
                        "entry_id": "4b9dc30c-f8dc-3ac7-a9f4-7a467c6014e1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An order for goods or services placed in advance."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ae101b3f-110c-35f2-aca6-95b77b3167a3",
                        "entry_id": "4b9dc30c-f8dc-3ac7-a9f4-7a467c6014e1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A binary relation that is reflexive and transitive."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d2395018-ebd0-43cf-9fd4-1d3b05f7dccb",
                                "sense_id": "ae101b3f-110c-35f2-aca6-95b77b3167a3",
                                "text": "The relation of logical implication over sentences is an example of a preorder."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cc45e9fb-6935-4a57-8e2a-51611a17ccd8",
                                "sense_id": "ae101b3f-110c-35f2-aca6-95b77b3167a3",
                                "text": "Example 436 For each set A, the relation #92;subseteq is a preorder on #92;mathcal#123;P#125;(A)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c156ca77-d885-470b-8709-13bfe91c5a09",
                                "sense_id": "ae101b3f-110c-35f2-aca6-95b77b3167a3",
                                "text": "Let us first compare S#95;1, S#95;2 and S#95;3 w.r.t. the partial preorder #92;succeq."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "64bcd639-092f-42b1-8f5e-2c131d4b3960",
                                "sense_id": "ae101b3f-110c-35f2-aca6-95b77b3167a3",
                                "text": "2000, Jean-Charles Pomerol, Sergio Barba-Romero, Multicriterion Decision in Management: Principles and Practice, Springer, Softcover, page 58,\nIt can easily be verified that the above relation is a preorder, i.e. that it is reflexive and transitive."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
